      "hints": [
        "Differentiate: $\\frac{dy}{dx} = -2(4x-3)^{-2} \\implies$ at $x = 1$, $m = -2$.",
        "Normal gradient is $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
        "Normal line: $y - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\implies y = \\frac{1}{2}x$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Differentiate $y = \\frac{1}{2}(4x - 3)^{-1}$ using the chain rule:\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{1}{2}(4x - 3)^{-2} \\times 4 = -2(4x - 3)^{-2} = -\\frac{2}{(4x - 3)^2}$$",
        "Evaluate the gradient of the curve and normal at $A\\left(1, \\, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$:\n$$\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{x=1} = -\\frac{2}{(4(1) - 3)^2} = -2 \\implies m_{\\text{normal}} = -\\frac{1}{-2} = \\frac{1}{2}$$",
        "Form and simplify the equation of the normal:\n$$y - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}(x - 1) \\implies y - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{1}{2} \\implies y = \\frac{1}{2}x$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
